<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ki Hajar Dewantara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Soewardi Soerjaningrat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ki Hajar Dewantara, born Raden Mas Soewardi Soerjaningrat into the Pakualaman princely family of Yogyakarta, was an Indonesian nationalist and educator. Exiled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> for his writings, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taman-siswa">Taman Siswa</a> school movement in 1922 and became independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s first minister of education. His birthday, 2 May, is National Education Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ki-hajar-dewantara.jpg" alt="Ki Hajar Dewantara" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Djawa Sjinboen Sja · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ki_Hadjar_Dewantara,_Djawa_Baroe_(1943),_p5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Soewardi was a grandson of Prince Paku Alam III through his father, GPH Soerjaningrat. His priyayi background gave him access to colonial education, which most of the population of the Indies could not enter. He finished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> primary school (ELS) and went on to STOVIA, a medical school for native students, but did not graduate because of illness. He then worked as a journalist for many newspapers, including De Expres, Kaoem Moeda and Poesara, with a popular, communicative style full of idealism for freedom and anti-colonial feeling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budi-utomo">Boedi Oetomo</a> from its founding in 1908 and organised its first congress in Yogyakarta. He also joined Insulinde, which advocated self-rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and the Indische Party of Ernest Douwes Dekker. In 1913 the colonial government collected money from the population, indigenous people included, for the centennial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Soewardi's column &quot;If I were a Dutchman&quot; (&quot;Als ik eens Nederlander was&quot;), printed in De Expres on 13 July 1913, criticised this fiercely. Governor General Alexander Idenburg ordered his arrest and exile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Bangka</a>. After protests by Douwes Dekker and Tjipto Mangoenkoesoemo, the three, later called the Tiga Serangkai, were exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> instead.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> he took part in the Indische Vereeniging and studied education, drawing on Fröbel, Montessori and the Tagore family's school at Santiniketan. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in September 1919, he helped his brother found a school in Yogyakarta, and in July 1922 he established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taman-siswa">Taman Siswa</a>, which provided education for the native population. He later dropped the title Raden Mas and took the name Ki Hadjar Dewantara. During the Japanese occupation he was a leader of Putera from 1943. In the first cabinet of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> he was Minister of Education and Culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in Yogyakarta on 26 April 1959 and was buried in the Taman Wijaya Brata cemetery. Presidential Decree no. 305 of 1959, dated 28 November 1959, declared him a national hero and made his birthday National Education Day. His maxim Tut Wuri Handayani, which asks those behind to give encouragement, is the motto of the Indonesian Ministry of Education. He held that education should be open to all regardless of sex, race, religion or social status. His portrait appeared on the 20,000 rupiah banknote in 1998, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taman-siswa">Taman Siswa</a> movement runs the Dewantara Kirti Griya Museum in Yogyakarta.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ki Hajar Dewantara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court was a centre of Javanese literature and arts, and the family produced <b>Ki Hajar Dewantara</b>, founder of the Taman Siswa school movement and pioneer of Indonesian national education.</blockquote><a href="/state/pakualaman">Pakualaman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yogyakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Yogyakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.8,110.39&amp;year=1934" aria-label="Yogyakarta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ki Hajar Dewantara"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4190 5665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4190 5665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.8,110.39&amp;year=1934">What was here in 1934? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
